1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,383
Ava: Imagine you know PyTorch well enough to build a model, but you want to contribute to its wider open source ecosystem.
Ava：假设你已经熟悉 PyTorch，能构建模型，也想为更广泛的开源生态做贡献。

2
00:00:07,383 --> 00:00:10,538
Finding a useful place to start can still be hard.
但找到一个有意义的切入点仍不容易。

3
00:00:10,538 --> 00:00:16,694
This announcement matters because it's about the people who help make that first connection.
这则公告重要，是因为它关注帮助人们迈出第一步的人。

4
00:00:16,694 --> 00:00:17,297
Brian: Exactly.
Brian：没错。

5
00:00:17,297 --> 00:00:31,023
The 2026 PyTorch Foundation Ambassador Program brings together community leaders who teach, mentor, organize events, create technical content, and contribute to open source projects.
2026 年 PyTorch 基金会大使计划汇聚了教授知识、指导新人、组织活动、创作技术内容并参与开源项目的社区领袖。

6
00:00:31,023 --> 00:00:40,348
The idea is to connect local communities with the wider Foundation ecosystem, so more people can learn and participate.
计划旨在连接本地社区与更广泛的基金会生态，让更多人学习和参与。

7
00:00:40,348 --> 00:00:43,810
Ava: I like that the starting point is a community need.
Ava：我喜欢它从社区需求出发。

8
00:00:43,810 --> 00:00:48,493
A framework engineer may read documentation and find a project alone.
框架工程师或许能靠阅读文档，自己找到项目。

9
00:00:48,493 --> 00:00:56,106
A student at a first workshop may need someone to explain what to try next and where questions are welcome.
第一次参加工作坊的学生，可能需要有人告诉他们下一步做什么、可以去哪里提问。

10
00:00:56,106 --> 00:00:58,770
Brian: And the article describes both paths.
Brian：文章也谈到了这两种路径。

11
00:00:58,770 --> 00:01:02,802
Someone new to PyTorch might attend a hands-on workshop.
PyTorch 新手可以参加实践工作坊。

12
00:01:02,802 --> 00:01:11,946
An experienced developer might discover another Foundation project, meet maintainers, or make a first open source contribution.
有经验的开发者可以发现基金会的其他项目、结识维护者，或首次为开源项目做贡献。

13
00:01:11,946 --> 00:01:17,480
Ambassadors help create those opportunities by bringing people together.
大使通过聚集人们来创造这些机会。

14
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,385
Ava: That changes the entry point.
Ava：这就改变了参与的起点。

15
00:01:19,385 --> 00:01:29,569
If I'm already comfortable with tensors and training loops, an introductory lesson may be less useful than a conversation about a project's open questions.
如果我已经熟悉张量和训练循环，讨论项目尚待解决的问题，可能比入门课程更有帮助。

16
00:01:29,569 --> 00:01:35,110
The program seems broad enough to serve both groups, though their needs differ.
这个计划似乎能兼顾两类人，尽管他们的需求不同。

17
00:01:35,110 --> 00:01:38,568
Brian: Right. The ambassadors' backgrounds are broad too.
Brian：对。大使们的背景也很广泛。

18
00:01:38,568 --> 00:01:50,394
The cohort includes people with experience in AI research, software and infrastructure engineering, technical education, open source maintenance, and community organizing.
这一届成员拥有 AI 研究、软件与基础设施工程、技术教育、开源维护和社区组织等领域的经验。

19
00:01:50,394 --> 00:01:58,836
Their work can involve AI systems and model training, or documentation, workshops, mentoring, and local applications.
他们的工作可能涉及 AI 系统和模型训练，也可能涉及文档、工作坊、指导新人和本地应用。

20
00:01:58,836 --> 00:02:02,489
Ava: So the useful contribution isn't always a code change.
Ava：所以有价值的贡献不一定是改代码。

21
00:02:02,489 --> 00:02:10,741
Documentation and a well-run workshop can help a developer understand a project well enough to use it or contribute later.
文档和办得好的工作坊能帮助开发者理解项目，日后使用它或参与贡献。

22
00:02:10,741 --> 00:02:16,706
That's especially relevant when the ecosystem has more than one project to explore.
当生态中有多个项目可供探索时，这一点尤其重要。

23
00:02:16,706 --> 00:02:17,009
Brian: Yes.
Brian：是的。

24
00:02:17,009 --> 00:02:24,278
The Foundation supports projects that help people build, train, optimize, and deploy AI systems.
基金会支持帮助人们构建、训练、优化和部署 AI 系统的项目。

25
00:02:24,278 --> 00:02:29,805
The article names PyTorch, vLLM, DeepSpeed, Ray, Helion, and Safetensors.
文章提到了 PyTorch、vLLM、DeepSpeed、Ray、Helion 和 Safetensors。

26
00:02:29,805 --> 00:02:39,544
Together, those examples span model training, inference, distributed computing, performance optimization, and model sharing.
这些项目涵盖模型训练、推理、分布式计算、性能优化和模型共享。

27
00:02:39,544 --> 00:02:44,524
Ava: I'd separate those two ideas. The list shows how wide the ecosystem is.
Ava：我会把这两点分开看。这份名单体现了生态的广度。

28
00:02:44,524 --> 00:02:48,381
It doesn't mean every ambassador teaches every project.
但这不意味着每位大使都会教授所有项目。

29
00:02:48,381 --> 00:02:57,078
A local event could focus on one topic that fits its audience, then point interested people toward the wider community.
本地活动可以聚焦适合受众的一个主题，再引导感兴趣的人走向更广泛的社区。

30
00:02:57,078 --> 00:03:00,843
Brian: That's consistent with the examples in the announcement.
Brian：这和公告中的例子一致。

31
00:03:00,843 --> 00:03:18,404
An ambassador might run an introductory PyTorch workshop for students, help developers explore scalable inference with vLLM, introduce engineers to distributed AI technologies, or write technical content that makes a difficult concept easier to understand.
大使可能为学生举办 PyTorch 入门工作坊，帮助开发者探索用 vLLM 实现可扩展推理，向工程师介绍分布式 AI 技术，或撰写技术内容，让难懂的概念更容易理解。

32
00:03:18,404 --> 00:03:20,220
Ava: Let's make that concrete.
Ava：举个具体例子。

33
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:24,940
Suppose a group of engineers is curious about scalable inference.
假设一群工程师对可扩展推理感兴趣。

34
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:29,515
A session around vLLM gives them a specific project to explore.
围绕 vLLM 的活动能让他们探索一个具体项目。

35
00:03:29,515 --> 00:03:41,194
The article doesn't describe a particular setup or performance result; the useful point here is the connection between a practical interest and a community.
文章没有介绍具体配置或性能结果；这里的关键是把实际兴趣与社区连接起来。

36
00:03:41,194 --> 00:03:47,099
Brian: And if the group is new to PyTorch, an introductory workshop serves a different purpose.
Brian：如果这群人刚接触 PyTorch，入门工作坊就有不同的作用。

37
00:03:47,099 --> 00:03:52,468
It can give people practical skills and a place to meet others who are learning.
它能让大家学到实用技能，也能结识其他学习者。

38
00:03:52,468 --> 00:03:58,656
The program also includes mentorship, which can help people keep moving after an event.
计划还包括导师指导，帮助人们在活动结束后继续前进。

39
00:03:58,656 --> 00:04:00,738
Ava: Here's the practical difference.
Ava：实际区别在这里。

40
00:04:00,738 --> 00:04:04,511
Reading about an unfamiliar project tells me what it does.
阅读陌生项目的资料，能让我知道它是做什么的。

41
00:04:04,511 --> 00:04:10,691
Meeting people around that project can show me where to ask questions and how to join the work.
结识项目相关的人，则能让我知道去哪里提问、如何参与。

42
00:04:10,691 --> 00:04:16,310
That second step matters when a developer wants to move from user to contributor.
开发者想从用户变成贡献者时，这一步很重要。

43
00:04:16,310 --> 00:04:29,175
Brian: The article frames that as one of the ambassadors' jobs: helping people discover Foundation projects, understand practical applications, and find opportunities to contribute.
Brian：文章把这视为大使的一项职责：帮助人们发现基金会项目、了解实际应用，并找到贡献的机会。

44
00:04:29,175 --> 00:04:32,724
It doesn't prescribe one route for every person.
文章没有为所有人规定同一条路径。

45
00:04:32,724 --> 00:04:39,580
Workshops, meetups, educational resources, and mentoring can each open a different door.
工作坊、线下聚会、教育资源和导师指导，都能提供不同的入口。

46
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:41,846
Ava: There's also a local part to this.
Ava：这里还有本地社区的作用。

47
00:04:41,846 --> 00:04:51,112
A meetup can respond to what its own community is interested in, while still connecting participants to projects and maintainers elsewhere.
聚会可以回应本地社区的兴趣，同时帮助参与者联系其他地方的项目和维护者。

48
00:04:51,112 --> 00:04:56,994
That seems useful for an ecosystem whose contributors live and work in many places.
这个生态的贡献者分布在各地，这样做似乎很有用。

49
00:04:56,994 --> 00:04:59,481
Brian: The cohort reflects that reach.
Brian：这一届大使也体现了这样的覆盖范围。

50
00:04:59,481 --> 00:05:10,313
For 2026, the Foundation welcomes 24 ambassadors across five program regions: Africa, Asia, Europe, the Middle East, and North America.
2026 年，基金会迎来 24 位大使，分布在五个计划区域：非洲、亚洲、欧洲、中东和北美。

51
00:05:10,313 --> 00:05:24,464
The article lists people connected to communities in countries including Nigeria, Burkina Faso, Singapore, India, Romania, Spain, the United Kingdom, and the United States.
文章列出的成员与尼日利亚、布基纳法索、新加坡、印度、罗马尼亚、西班牙、英国和美国等国的社区有联系。

52
00:05:24,464 --> 00:05:30,695
Ava: That's a clear result we can report: who's in the cohort and how widely the program is organized.
Ava：有一个明确的结果可以报告：这届大使有哪些人，以及项目覆盖了多广的范围。

53
00:05:30,695 --> 00:05:32,750
But it isn't an adoption metric.
但这不是衡量采用情况的指标。

54
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:40,294
We shouldn't turn the number of ambassadors into a claim about how many new contributors the projects will gain.
我们不能拿大使人数来推断这些项目会新增多少贡献者。

55
00:05:40,294 --> 00:05:40,763
Brian: Agreed.
Brian：同意。

56
00:05:40,763 --> 00:05:46,921
The article describes what these people bring and what they hope to do over the coming year.
文章介绍了这些人的经验，以及他们希望在未来一年做什么。

57
00:05:46,921 --> 00:05:50,201
It doesn't establish future participation totals.
它没有给出未来的参与人数。

58
00:05:50,201 --> 00:05:58,116
We can talk about the opportunities the program aims to create without treating those plans as measured outcomes.
我们可以谈项目打算创造哪些机会，但不能把计划当成已经取得的成果。

59
00:05:58,116 --> 00:06:02,090
Ava: The ambassadors' reflections make those aims less abstract.
Ava：大使们的感想让这些目标更具体了。

60
00:06:02,090 --> 00:06:12,733
One ambassador from Turkey describes contributing to PyTorch documentation and realizing that a small contribution could become part of something much bigger.
一位来自土耳其的大使说，她曾为 PyTorch 文档做贡献，并意识到小小的贡献也能成为更宏大事业的一部分。

61
00:06:12,733 --> 00:06:18,674
That's a useful reminder for an engineer who assumes only large code changes count.
这提醒工程师：并非只有大规模代码改动才算贡献。

62
00:06:18,674 --> 00:06:27,644
Brian: She hopes to help others feel that sense of belonging through practical learning, welcoming communities, and chances to contribute.
Brian：她希望通过实践学习、友好的社区和贡献机会，让更多人感受到归属感。

63
00:06:27,644 --> 00:06:30,793
That gives documentation work a human context.
这也让文档工作有了人的温度。

64
00:06:30,793 --> 00:06:37,648
It can help someone enter a project, and the act of contributing can help them feel part of it.
文档能帮助人们参与项目，而贡献本身也能让他们感到自己是其中一员。

65
00:06:37,648 --> 00:06:44,944
Ava: Another ambassador, from Egypt, says open source communities taught her much of what she knows about building AI.
Ava：另一位来自埃及的大使说，开源社区教会了她许多构建 AI 的知识。

66
00:06:44,944 --> 00:06:53,622
She wants to give back through teaching and mentoring, and bring more engineers from Egypt and Africa into the PyTorch ecosystem.
她想通过教学和指导回馈社区，帮助更多埃及和非洲的工程师加入 PyTorch 生态。

67
00:06:53,622 --> 00:07:00,656
Brian: That example explains why the program includes people who teach as well as people who maintain software.
Brian：这个例子说明，项目为什么既吸纳教书育人的人，也吸纳维护软件的人。

68
00:07:00,656 --> 00:07:08,908
If learning in a community helped an engineer build skills, teaching those skills can make the next person's entry easier.
如果工程师从社区学习并提升了技能，再把这些技能教给别人，就能让后来者更容易入门。

69
00:07:08,908 --> 00:07:14,804
The article presents that as an ambassador's motivation, not as a measured effect.
文章说的是一位大使的愿望，而不是已经测得的效果。

70
00:07:14,804 --> 00:07:20,535
Ava: The third reflection is especially interesting for someone following inference work.
Ava：第三位大使的感想，对关注推理工作的人尤其有意思。

71
00:07:20,535 --> 00:07:29,063
An ambassador connected to Singapore and Malaysia wants hands-on education and mentorship to help more developers contribute.
一位与新加坡和马来西亚有联系的大使，希望通过实践教育和指导，帮助更多开发者参与贡献。

72
00:07:29,063 --> 00:07:36,850
He also wants to bring the PyTorch and vLLM communities closer together in Singapore and across Asia-Pacific.
他还希望拉近新加坡及亚太地区 PyTorch 和 vLLM 社区之间的距离。

73
00:07:36,850 --> 00:07:41,964
Brian: That gives us a specific example of a bridge between project communities.
Brian：这给出了一个连接不同项目社区的具体例子。

74
00:07:41,964 --> 00:07:46,728
The announcement doesn't say what form that collaboration will take.
公告没有说明这种合作会采取什么形式。

75
00:07:46,728 --> 00:07:56,256
It does tell us what he hopes to encourage: learning, contribution, and closer contact between people interested in those projects.
但它说明了他希望促进什么：学习、贡献，以及对这些项目感兴趣的人之间更密切的交流。

76
00:07:56,256 --> 00:07:59,248
Ava: I can see a pattern across those reflections.
Ava：这些感想有一个共同点。

77
00:07:59,248 --> 00:08:08,356
One person starts from documentation, another from teaching what a community taught her, and another from connecting project communities.
有人从文档入手，有人把从社区学到的知识教给别人，还有人致力于连接不同项目的社区。

78
00:08:08,356 --> 00:08:13,958
Those are different activities, but each gives someone else a way to take part.
做法各不相同，但都为别人提供了参与的途径。

79
00:08:13,958 --> 00:08:17,650
Brian: Yes, and the wider cohort brings still more experience.
Brian：是的，其他大使也带来了更多不同的经验。

80
00:08:17,650 --> 00:08:23,424
The article mentions research, infrastructure, applications, and community organizing.
文章提到了研究、基础设施、应用开发和社区组织工作。

81
00:08:23,424 --> 00:08:34,180
It doesn't assign every activity to every ambassador, so the safest reading is that the program gathers people with different skills and local connections.
文章并没有说每位大使都会做所有这些事。稳妥的理解是，这个项目汇集了技能和本地社区联系各不相同的人。

82
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:35,916
Ava: That's a useful boundary.
Ava：这个界限很有用。

83
00:08:35,916 --> 00:08:44,529
For a technical listener, it's tempting to ask which project will get which workshop, or how many contributions will follow.
技术领域的听众可能会想知道，哪个项目会举办什么工作坊，之后又会产生多少贡献。

84
00:08:44,529 --> 00:08:47,724
Those would be valuable things to learn later.
这些都值得以后关注。

85
00:08:47,724 --> 00:08:52,242
This announcement is about the cohort and its intended work.
这份公告讲的是这届大使及其计划开展的工作。

86
00:08:52,242 --> 00:08:56,150
Brian: And intended work still tells us something concrete.
Brian：计划中的工作仍然透露了具体信息。

87
00:08:56,150 --> 00:09:08,624
Over the coming year, ambassadors will help create opportunities for developers, researchers, students, and open source enthusiasts to learn, connect, and contribute.
未来一年，大使们将为开发者、研究人员、学生和开源爱好者创造学习、交流和贡献的机会。

88
00:09:08,624 --> 00:09:18,368
The activities described include technical education, community events, mentorship, and introductions to Foundation projects.
文章提到的活动包括技术教育、社区活动、指导，以及介绍基金会旗下的项目。

89
00:09:18,368 --> 00:09:19,833
Ava: So what happens next?
Ava：接下来会怎样？

90
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:28,271
If I follow this as an engineer, I'd look for the workshops, resources, and community initiatives the ambassadors create.
如果我是关注此事的工程师，我会留意大使们推出的工作坊、资源和社区活动。

91
00:09:28,271 --> 00:09:34,270
Those would show how the broad goal turns into something people can actually use.
这些能让我们看到，宏观目标如何变成人们真正用得上的东西。

92
00:09:34,270 --> 00:09:35,076
Brian: That's fair.
Brian：有道理。

93
00:09:35,076 --> 00:09:44,277
The article says the Foundation looks forward to what the ambassadors will teach, organize, create, and contribute during the year ahead.
文章说，基金会期待看到大使们在未来一年教授、组织、创造和贡献的成果。

94
00:09:44,277 --> 00:09:50,268
It gives us a direction, while leaving the exact events and outcomes for the future.
它指出了方向，具体活动和成效则要留待日后揭晓。

95
00:09:50,268 --> 00:09:52,038
Ava: Where does that leave us?
Ava：那我们该如何理解这件事？

96
00:09:52,038 --> 00:10:00,532
I'd take away that the Foundation's ecosystem includes several kinds of AI work, and people need different ways into it.
我的看法是，基金会的生态涵盖多种 AI 工作，人们需要不同的参与途径。

97
00:10:00,532 --> 00:10:08,810
A local introduction, useful documentation, or contact with a maintainer can each help someone find a next step.
本地引导、实用文档，或与维护者建立联系，都能帮助人们找到下一步。

98
00:10:08,810 --> 00:10:19,118
Brian: And I'd keep the scale in view: 24 ambassadors across five program regions, with backgrounds ranging from engineering to education and community work.
Brian：我也会记住它的规模：24 位大使，覆盖五个项目区域，背景从工程、教育到社区工作。

99
00:10:19,118 --> 00:10:25,853
The program connects those people to learning and contribution across PyTorch Foundation projects.
这个项目让这些人围绕 PyTorch 基金会的各个项目，推动学习和贡献。

100
00:10:25,853 --> 00:10:31,288
What comes from those connections will become clearer as the year unfolds.
随着这一年展开，这些联系会带来什么，将逐渐明朗。

101
00:10:31,288 --> 00:10:33,363
Ava: That's the useful takeaway for me.
Ava：这就是我觉得最有用的收获。

102
00:10:33,363 --> 00:10:37,940
If you're already building with PyTorch, there's more to explore around it.
如果你已经在用 PyTorch 开发，还有更多相关内容值得探索。

103
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:45,126
And if you want to contribute, the community connections may help you find a place where your skills are useful.
如果你想贡献力量，社区的人脉也许能帮你找到发挥所长的地方。

104
00:10:45,126 --> 00:10:46,907
Brian: We'll leave it there.
Brian：今天就聊到这里。

105
00:10:46,907 --> 00:10:57,337
The 2026 cohort is in place, and the practical work lies ahead: teaching, organizing, creating, and helping others join in.
2026 届成员已经就位，接下来要做的是教学、组织、创作，帮助更多人参与。

106
00:10:57,337 --> 00:10:59,468
Thanks for listening.
感谢收听。
